{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkBSanNgQZSyAjZ9LXSi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`contract/claim.compact` is written and compiles cleanly.\n\n- **Names and types:** I used `witness secretKey(): Bytes<32>`, `export pure circuit publicKey(sk)`, `export circuit claim(): []` and the public counter `claims`. There are no other witnesses.\n- **Claim record:** `claim()` stores a nullifier in a public `Set`, so the `Set` is the only per-user data on the ledger. It asserts the nullifier isn't already in the set, inserts it, then increments `claims`. A second claim with the same secret key fails with \"Already claimed\".\n- **Privacy:** The nullifier is a hash of the secret key under the domain tag `\"claim:nf:\"`. The public key uses a different tag, `\"claim:pk:\"`. Since the two hashes differ, the ledger's nullifiers can't be matched against public keys. Someone who has a public key can't compute that user's nullifier, so they can't tell whether that user has claimed. Storing the public key itself, or a hash of it, would have leaked exactly that.\n- **Disclosure:** The nullifier is the only value passed through `disclose()`. The secret key and public key stay private.\n\nI compiled it with `compact compile` but didn't write or run any tests."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":162,"cache_read_input_tokens":21768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":162},"output_tokens":413,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"315e94e6-c9a3-4ab0-82d8-1079113d93ef","uuid":"e7c4f12a-2f67-4525-ac90-fb212eb40b9e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T17:32:45.007Z","request_id":"req_011CgkBSaYjStTiSLk2HDu7v"}
